cle is ONE subcommand owning the find→open/comment/close branch decision (branch logic in Python, unit-tested) or FOUR `if:`-gated thin steps (branch decision stays as YAML expressions), and reconcile Approach.1, Scope, and the DRY_RUN/`if:`-stays-in-YAML statement so the data-flow across the find→act step boundary is unambiguous.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"advisory","drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Approach.1: 'heartbeat-alert: the find/open/comment/close lifecycle ... one function per transition, dedup pattern preserved'","Scope: 'Find/Open/Comment/Close heartbeat alert' listed as separate replaced steps; 'the step-level `if: env.DRY_RUN == 'false'` gates stay in YAML'","YAML: open/comment/close steps are separately `if:`-gated on `steps.check.outputs.stale` AND `steps.find_ticket.outputs.ticket_id` (lines 222-226, 260-264, 278-282), and find_ticket writes ticket_id to GITHUB_OUTPUT (line 220) consumed across a step boundary","Testing lists per-transition unit cases ('open-when-none, dedup-when-open, comment-when-still-stale, close-on-recovery') which only exist if the branch decision lives in Python"],"finding":"The plan specifies two structurally different mechanisms for the alert lifecycle without resolving which to build: Approach.1 describes a single `heartbeat-alert` subcommand that does find/open/comment/close ('one function per transition'), while Scope lists FOUR separate thin invocations ('Find/Open/Comment/Close heartbeat alert') and states the step-level `if:` gates stay in YAML. If the four `if:`-gated steps stay, the find result must round-trip ticket_id through GITHUB_OUTPUT across a step boundary (so the stale-vs-ticket_id branch decisions remain YAML expressions, not the Python this migration exists to unit-test); if it is one subcommand, the branch logic moves into Python but per-step `if:` gating and step-outcome parity change shape. The data-flow differs between the two and the implementer cannot tell which is intended.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fc307595497983f13","impact":0.536,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach step 1 (heartbeat-alert / check-binding-drift+binding-drift-alert) vs Scope bullet '10 logic steps -> thin invocations'","norm_id":"n4da440f3974a5296","priority":0.402,"reason":"default-advisory","scenarios":["Implementer builds one all-in-one subcommand but leaves the four YAML `if:` gates, so open/comment/close double-decide and the gates race the subcommand's own branch","Implementer keeps four thin steps but the stale/ticket_id branch decisions stay in YAML expressions, so the classification logic this ticket set out to unit-test is not actually extracted"],"severity":"major","suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.75,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"module","debt_impact":"medium","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"medium","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"medium","prod_impact":"low","reversibility":"moderate","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"medium"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"","cohort":null,"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"dropped","drop_reason":null,"evidence":[],"finding":"The plan does not carry the current check-binding-drift exit-code contract into the migrated subcommand: `rebar bridge-fsck` exits 1 precisely WHEN it finds drift (the condition the canary exists to detect), and the YAML deliberately ignores that exit via `|| true` while swallowing JSON-parse failures into an empty-data default. The plan's error-handling narrative states the opposite rule ('a failing rebar invocation FAILS LOUD -> red canary run') for the alert subcommands and details only check-heartbeat's api-error/transient handling, leaving check-binding-drift's fail-soft-on-exit-1 semantics unspecified.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f8a4c783564b2a0e2","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach step 1 (check-binding-drift subcommand); Error handling paragraph under heartbeat-alert","norm_id":"n6fca81519cbb108c","priority":0.0,"reason":"veto:absence-refuted","scenarios":[],"severity":"none","suggested_fix":"In Approach step 1, add to the check-binding-drift description: 'bridge-fsck returns non-zero WHEN drift exists — the subcommand must classify on stdout JSON regardless of exit code (as the current `|| true` does) and must NOT fail-loud on a non-zero exit; only an unparseable/absent JSON payload degrades to drift_found=false. The FAIL-LOUD contract applies to the write subcommands (heartbeat-alert / binding-drift-alert), not the read-only classifiers.' Add a Testing bullet: 'check-binding-drift: bridge-fsck exit=1 with a non-empty binding_drift section -> drift_found=true (exit-code-is-not-error)'.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.3125,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"no","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"na","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"no","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"no","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"no","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"insufficient"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"none","divergent_implementation":"no